<drill/D-BAB-13> a atlas:Drill ;
    atlas:answer "70.35, so 70. The linear ramp from 0 to 204 between two voxel centres is 14 slices long; its alpha is already opaque before the full 204 is reached, so the pixel is the colour of the ramp, not the block. With nearest filtering (8 voxels) there is no ramp and the pixel is 204." ;
    atlas:checkedOn "2026-11-15"^^xsd:date ;
    atlas:checkedText "2026-11-15 python3 -I (70.35); the rig gives 70 in both engines" ;
    dcterms:source <https://github.com/hagopjay/x3d.docs/blob/ff0565e5a248ca391d68527716a58a821f25d152/docs/learn/08-babylon-bridge.md?plain=1#L940> ;
    atlas:inChapter <chapter/08> ;
    rdfs:label "D-BAB-13 Why a block of 204 draws 70" ;
    atlas:level "L3" ;
    atlas:line 940 ;
    prov:wasQuotedFrom <doc/x3d.docs@ff0565e/docs/learn/08-babylon-bridge.md> ;
    atlas:status "ran" ;
    atlas:task "A 16 cubed volume of dimensions 0.8 has voxels of 204 in the middle 8 along the viewing axis and 0 outside, linear filtering. X_ITE blends 400 slices spaced 0.8 x sqrt(3) / 399 apart with source alpha, no correction, the default ramp g = (v x 256 / 255 - 0.5 / 255) giving colour g and alpha g. Predict the centre pixel (0 to 255), and say why it is not 204." ;
    atlas:title "Why a block of 204 draws 70" ;
    atlas:verify "a loop over the slices front to back (pixel += (1 - A) x g x g; A += (1 - A) x g), stopping at A > 0.998." .

