<drill/D-PHY-3> a atlas:Drill ;
    atlas:answer "pre-projection violation falls about 23x, 16x, 25x per step (7.3e-3, 3.2e-4, 2.0e-5, 8.2e-7); energy lost by t = 10 s is 8.79, 4.36, 1.46, 0.31 J/kg (the swing amplitude is g R = 9.81). Read after the projection, the violation would be about 1e-16 for every row (the review's point). The review's numbers differ (2.4e-2 at 1 substep) because its bead, start and time differ; the trend is the claim." ;
    atlas:checkedOn "2026-11-14"^^xsd:date ;
    atlas:checkedText "`2026-11-14 python3 -I numpy 2.5.3`: 7.31e-03, 3.19e-04, 2.03e-05, 8.16e-07; dE -8.7945, -4.3632, -1.4550, -0.3125." ;
    dcterms:source <https://github.com/hagopjay/x3d.docs/blob/ff0565e5a248ca391d68527716a58a821f25d152/docs/learn/13-physics.md?plain=1#L491> ;
    atlas:inChapter <chapter/13> ;
    rdfs:label "D-PHY-3 XPBD bead on a wire: substeps against residual and energy" ;
    atlas:level "L2" ;
    atlas:line 491 ;
    prov:wasQuotedFrom <doc/x3d.docs@ff0565e/docs/learn/13-physics.md> ;
    atlas:status "ran" ;
    atlas:task "A unit-mass bead starts at rest at (1, 0) on a circular wire of radius 1, gravity -y, 60 frames per second, 10 s. Each substep: predict, then project onto the circle, then set `v = (x - x_prev)/h`. Record the worst violation **before** projection and the final energy change for 1, 5, 20, 100 substeps per frame." ;
    atlas:title "XPBD bead on a wire: substeps against residual and energy" ;
    atlas:verify "```python def bead(sub, frame=1/60, T=10, R=1.0): h = frame/sub; x = np.array([R, 0.0]); v = np.zeros(2) E = lambda x, v: 0.5*(v@v) + g*x[1] E0 = E(x, v); worst = 0 for _ in range(int(T/frame)): for _ in range(sub): xp = x.copy(); v = v + h*np.array([0, -g]); x = x + h*v worst = max(worst, abs(math.sqrt(x@x) - R)) x = x*(R/math.sqrt(x@x)); v = (x - xp)/h return worst, E(x, v) - E0 for s in (1, 5, 20, 100): print(s, *bead(s)) ```" .

<drill/D-PHY-4> a atlas:Drill ;
    atlas:answer "(a) KE end is 0.500000 for fixed, buggy and none. (b) The exchange test: at t = 1.0 s the fixed rule has the struck disc moving at 1.0 and the cue at 0; the buggy rule and no collision leave the struck disc at rest (the cue passes through it; the buggy rule exchanges only after the discs pass each other). Oblique: tangential components unchanged, normal components swapped, so the outgoing velocities are perpendicular (dot about -2e-16, failure mode 148). Because this scene is axis-aligned, it is the weak form; failure mode 151 asks for the oblique one." ;
    atlas:checkedOn "2026-11-14"^^xsd:date ;
    atlas:checkedText "`2026-11-14 python3 -I numpy 2.5.3`: KE 0.500000 three times; v at t = 1.0 s fixed [0,0] and [1,0]; buggy [1,0] and [0,0]; none [1,0] and [0,0]. The oblique extension was not run." ;
    dcterms:source <https://github.com/hagopjay/x3d.docs/blob/ff0565e5a248ca391d68527716a58a821f25d152/docs/learn/13-physics.md?plain=1#L511> ;
    atlas:inChapter <chapter/13> ;
    rdfs:label "D-PHY-4 A check that cannot fail: the wrong twin" ;
    atlas:level "L3" ;
    atlas:line 511 ;
    atlas:mentions <fm/148>, <fm/151> ;
    prov:wasQuotedFrom <doc/x3d.docs@ff0565e/docs/learn/13-physics.md> ;
    atlas:status "ran" ;
    atlas:task "Two equal discs of diameter 1, mass 1; the cue moves at 1 m/s into the other at rest, head-on. Implement the collision with the correct trigger (approaching, `vrel > 0`) and with the inverted trigger (separating, `vrel < 0`), plus no collisions. (a) Show kinetic energy at the end is 0.5 in all three. (b) Find a test that tells them apart. Extend: write the oblique version and state the expected outgoing angle." ;
    atlas:title "A check that cannot fail: the wrong twin" ;
    atlas:verify "```python def run(rule, steps=1500, h=1e-3): p = np.array([[-1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 0.0]]); v = np.array([[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 0.0]]) for i in range(steps): p += h*v d = p[1]-p[0]; dist = np.linalg.norm(d) if dist < 1.0 and rule != 'none': n = d/dist; vr = (v[0]-v[1])@n if (vr > 0) if rule == 'fixed' else (vr < 0): dv = vr*n; v[0] -= dv; v[1] += dv if i == 1000: v_at = v.copy() return v, v_at for r in ('fixed', 'buggy', 'none'): vf, v1 = run(r); print(r, 0.5*np.sum(vf*vf), v1[0], v1[1]) ```" .

<drill/D-PHY-5> a atlas:Drill ;
    atlas:answer "T = 1: 1.00 and 1.00. T = 2 pi: explicit 1.06, symplectic 2.00. A first-order method measures second order at one full period because the leading error term cancels; the v6 receipt reports 2.0 and 1.10 for the same effect (explicit 1.06 here against 1.10 there: the step-size grids differ)." ;
    atlas:checkedOn "2026-11-14"^^xsd:date ;
    atlas:checkedText "`2026-11-14 python3 -I numpy 2.5.3`: T=1 explicit 1.00, symplectic 1.00; T=2pi explicit 1.06, symplectic 2.00." ;
    dcterms:source <https://github.com/hagopjay/x3d.docs/blob/ff0565e5a248ca391d68527716a58a821f25d152/docs/learn/13-physics.md?plain=1#L533> ;
    atlas:inChapter <chapter/13> ;
    rdfs:label "D-PHY-5 Order of a method depends on where you measure" ;
    atlas:level "L2" ;
    atlas:line 533 ;
    prov:wasQuotedFrom <doc/x3d.docs@ff0565e/docs/learn/13-physics.md> ;
    atlas:status "ran" ;
    atlas:task "Integrate the harmonic oscillator `x'' = -x` (x0 = 1, v0 = 0) with explicit and symplectic Euler to T = 1 and to T = 2 pi at 50, 100, 200, 400, 800 steps. Fit the log-log slope of the phase-space error `hypot(x - cos T, v + sin T)` against h." ;
    atlas:title "Order of a method depends on where you measure" ;
    atlas:usesTool <tool/symplectic@1.00>, <tool/symplectic@2.00> ;
    atlas:verify "```python def ho(m, h, T): x, v = 1.0, 0.0 for _ in range(int(round(T/h))): if m == 'explicit': x, v = x + h*v, v - h*x else: v -= h*x; x += h*v return x, v for T in (1.0, 2*math.pi): for m in ('explicit', 'symplectic'): hs = [T/k for k in (50, 100, 200, 400, 800)] errs = [math.hypot(ho(m, h, T)[0]-math.cos(T), ho(m, h, T)[1]+math.sin(T)) for h in hs] print(T, m, np.polyfit(np.log(hs), np.log(errs), 1)[0]) ```" .

<drill/D-PHY-6> a atlas:Drill ;
    atlas:answer "at 0.60, 0.70 and 0.7071 the amplitude stays near 1e-3 to 5e-3; at 0.72 it is 1.2e59 and at 0.80 4.4e170. Stability needs `c^2 dt^2 lambda_max <= 4`; for the 5-point Laplacian lambda_max/dx^2 tends to 8 in two dimensions, giving `c dt/dx <= 1/sqrt(2) = 0.7071` (0.7082 on the finite 28 x 28 grid per the review's bisection, because the finite grid's largest eigenvalue is a bit under 8). The wave speed in cells per step (0.72) is under one, so \"outrunning a cell\" cannot be the cause. The bisection to 0.7082 was **not** run here." ;
    atlas:checkedOn "2026-11-14"^^xsd:date ;
    atlas:checkedText "`2026-11-14 python3 -I numpy 2.5.3`: 0.6 -> 2.69e-03; 0.70 -> 3.42e-03; 0.7071 -> 4.65e-03; 0.72 -> 1.18e+59; 0.80 -> 4.40e+170. Bisection not run." ;
    dcterms:source <https://github.com/hagopjay/x3d.docs/blob/ff0565e5a248ca391d68527716a58a821f25d152/docs/learn/13-physics.md?plain=1#L553> ;
    atlas:inChapter <chapter/13> ;
    rdfs:label "D-PHY-6 The CFL limit is a stability limit, not \"outrunning a cell\"" ;
    atlas:level "L3" ;
    atlas:line 553 ;
    prov:wasQuotedFrom <doc/x3d.docs@ff0565e/docs/learn/13-physics.md> ;
    atlas:status "ran" ;
    atlas:task "Run the 2D wave equation `v += (c dt/dx)^2 * laplacian(u); u += v` (symplectic Euler, 5-point Laplacian, fixed boundaries) on a 28 x 28 grid with a 1e-3 random start, 400 steps, at c dt/dx = 0.60, 0.70, 0.7071, 0.72, 0.80. Then bisect the limit and explain it with the largest Laplacian eigenvalue." ;
    atlas:title "The CFL limit is a stability limit, not \"outrunning a cell\"" ;
    atlas:verify "```python def wave(cfl, N=28, steps=400): u = np.zeros((N, N)); vv = np.zeros((N, N)); rng = np.random.default_rng(1) u[1:-1, 1:-1] = rng.normal(size=(N-2, N-2))*1e-3; k = cfl*cfl for _ in range(steps): lap = np.zeros_like(u) lap[1:-1, 1:-1] = u[2:, 1:-1]+u[:-2, 1:-1]+u[1:-1, 2:]+u[1:-1, :-2]-4*u[1:-1, 1:-1] vv += k*lap; u += vv; u[0] = u[-1] = 0; u[:, 0] = u[:, -1] = 0 return np.abs(u).max() for c in (0.6, 0.70, 0.7071, 0.72, 0.8): print(c, wave(c)) ```" .

<drill/D-PHY-7> a atlas:Drill ;
    atlas:answer "jitter 0: dE/E0 0.0002, final angle 0.007586 for both seeds (the seed is unused, so bit-identical). Jitter 0.5: dE/E0 -0.0117, final angle 0.1592 against 0.4356 for the two seeds. Same file, same nominal step, different runs: with a render-locked variable step, a hash replay across hosts is impossible by construction." ;
    atlas:checkedOn "2026-11-14"^^xsd:date ;
    atlas:checkedText "`2026-11-14 python3 -I numpy 2.5.3`: jitter 0 dE/E0 0.0002 theta 0.007586 twice; jitter 0.5 dE/E0 -0.0117, theta 0.159239 and 0.435642." ;
    dcterms:source <https://github.com/hagopjay/x3d.docs/blob/ff0565e5a248ca391d68527716a58a821f25d152/docs/learn/13-physics.md?plain=1#L571> ;
    atlas:inChapter <chapter/13> ;
    rdfs:label "D-PHY-7 Fixed against variable step" ;
    atlas:level "L2" ;
    atlas:line 571 ;
    prov:wasQuotedFrom <doc/x3d.docs@ff0565e/docs/learn/13-physics.md> ;
    atlas:status "ran" ;
    atlas:task "Symplectic-Euler pendulum, 10,000 steps of nominal h = 0.01. Run with jitter 0 and with each step drawn uniformly from `h (1 +- 0.5)`, for two random seeds. Compare energy change and final angle." ;
    atlas:title "Fixed against variable step" ;
    atlas:verify "```python def vs(jit, seed): rng = np.random.default_rng(seed); th, w = 1.0, 0.0 E0 = 0.5*w*w + g*(1-math.cos(th)) for _ in range(10000): h = 0.01*(1+jit*rng.uniform(-1, 1)); w += h*(-g*math.sin(th)); th += h*w return (0.5*w*w + g*(1-math.cos(th)) - E0)/E0, th for j in (0, 0.5): print(j, vs(j, 0), vs(j, 1)) ```" .

