<drill/D-UNR-5> a atlas:Drill ;
    atlas:answer "offset 3 gives 38.63 dB and noise sigma 5 gives 34.31 dB (seeded as in the check). A black 8 x 8 square on the same seeded image (1.6 % of the pixels) gives MSE 348.6 and 22.71 dB, lower than both, so here PSNR does rank it worse; but the same square on a dark image would score far higher, and the offset pair, which is wrong everywhere, scores best. The point is that PSNR cannot tell a localised wrong object from a global exposure shift. That is why the rig also reports a geometry-only delta and why FLIP's error map is kept." ;
    atlas:checkedOn "2026-11-14"^^xsd:date ;
    atlas:checkedText "2026-11-14 numpy for all three numbers (offset 38.63 dB, noise 34.31 dB, square 22.71 dB)." ;
    dcterms:source <https://github.com/hagopjay/x3d.docs/blob/ff0565e5a248ca391d68527716a58a821f25d152/docs/learn/12-unreal-tier1.md?plain=1#L556> ;
    atlas:inChapter <chapter/12> ;
    rdfs:label "D-UNR-5 Break a PSNR comparison" ;
    atlas:level "L3" ;
    atlas:line 556 ;
    prov:wasQuotedFrom <doc/x3d.docs@ff0565e/docs/learn/12-unreal-tier1.md> ;
    atlas:status "ran" ;
    atlas:task "make two 64 x 64 random RGB images that differ by a constant offset of 3 and two that differ by Gaussian noise of sigma 5. Compute the PSNR of each. Then construct a third pair, identical except for one 8 x 8 square set to black, whose PSNR is higher than the offset pair's. What does that say about using PSNR to rank engines?" ;
    atlas:title "Break a PSNR comparison" ;
    atlas:verify "numpy; PSNR = `10 log10(255² / mean((A-B)²))`." .

